  "notes": "90s-revivalist female country — steel, fiddle and cry-in-the-voice phrasing rebuilt with modern clarity. The drums are a deliberate reduction and the entry frames them that way: \"cross-stick verses with brushes or light shaker, snare entering at the chorus, kick on 1 and 3, low fill density; the uptempo cuts move toward a two-beat. Deliberately smaller and drier than her 2010s peers\" — so the velocity tiers, the perc gain and the fill count all sit under the modern cohort's while the hat opens on beat 4 for the two-beat. Harmony carries a period habit the entry names explicitly: \"the 90s-revival habit of a V7 before the chorus,\" which is the raised seventh share against otherwise plain I–IV–V and I–V–vi–IV, plus the i–VI–III–VII of the documented minor-tonic duet. The counter is the entry's headline claim — \"pedal steel and fiddle restored to lead-voice status\" — and \"no synth pads,\" so the counter is an answer lick rather than a pad. Melody is \"mid-alto with a deliberate country break/cry at phrase ends, narrow verse range, chorus a sixth up, minimal melisma.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"palette and lane clear; tempo evidence thin (two catalogue points) [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
